<!DOCTYPE html><html><head><title>IRC channel logs</title><style>html {
  background: #fdfdfd;
}

h1 {
  font-weight: 300;
}

h2 {
  font-weight: 200;
}

h3 {
  padding: .5em 0;
  border-top: 3px dotted #ddd;
  margin-bottom: 0;
}

form {
  width: 400px;
  display: flex;
}

input {
  width: 100%;
  display: flex;
  border-radius: .25em 0 0 .25em;
  border: 1px solid #aaa;
  border-right: 0;
  padding: 0.5em;
}

button {
  display: flex;
  border-radius: 0 .25em .25em 0;
  background-color: #007bff;
  border: 1px solid #007bff;
  padding: .5em;
  cursor: pointer;
  color: white;
}

button:hover {
  background-color: #0069d9;
  border-color: #0062cc;
}

a {
  color: #007bff;
  text-decoration: none;
}

a:hover {
  text-decoration: underline;
}

h4 {
  margin-bottom: .5em;
}

table td {
  padding: 0.75em;
}

table tr:hover {
  background: #eee;
}

.year {
  display: table;
}

.month {
  display: table-cell;
  padding-right: 1em;
}

ul {
  margin: 0;
  padding: 0;
  list-style: none;
}

.nick {
  padding-right: 0.6rem;
  font-weight: bold;
  text-align: right;
  width: 13rem;
  display: table-cell;
}

.nick a {
  color: inherit;
  text-decoration: none;
}

.message {
  display: table-cell;
  padding-left: 0.6rem;
  border-left: 2px solid #333;
}

.notice {
  color: #859900;
  font-style: italic;
}

.line {
  line-height: 1.8rem;
  display: table;
}

#logs {
  margin-top: 1.5rem;
  padding: 1.5rem;
}
</style></head><body><h1>IRC channel logs</h1><h2>2024-12-03.log</h2><p><a href="/bootstrappable">back to list of logs</a></p><div id="logs"><div class="line" id="030439"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#030439" label="[03:04:39]">&lt;fossy_&gt;</a></span><span class="message">stikonas: i'm re-investigating some of the kaem environment variable weirdness on bare metal in stage0-posix that I was running into</span></div><div class="line" id="030509"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#030509" label="[03:05:09]">&lt;fossy_&gt;</a></span><span class="message">i'm not certain that the use of get_next_variable_name in _get_environmental_variables (uefi.c in M2libc) is compliant</span></div><div class="line" id="030613"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#030613" label="[03:06:13]">&lt;fossy_&gt;</a></span><span class="message">(well, more i cannot figure out if it is)</span></div><div class="line" id="030734"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#030734" label="[03:07:34]">&lt;fossy_&gt;</a></span><span class="message">i'm trying to interpret the spec, what does it mean by &quot; If a VariableName buffer</span></div><div class="line" id="030736"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#030736" label="[03:07:36]">&lt;fossy_&gt;</a></span><span class="message">on input is not a Null-terminated string, EFI_INVALID_PARAMETER is returned&quot;?</span></div><div class="line" id="030748"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#030748" label="[03:07:48]">&lt;fossy_&gt;</a></span><span class="message">particularly, what does &quot;on input&quot; mean?</span></div><div class="line" id="031151"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#031151" label="[03:11:51]">&lt;fossy_&gt;</a></span><span class="message">it sounds like VariableName should be a null-terminated string always, and if it is an empty null-terminated string passed in, then it restarts the search??</span></div><div class="line" id="031309"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#031309" label="[03:13:09]">&lt;fossy_&gt;</a></span><span class="message">also, where does the magic numbers in EFI_SHELL_VARIABLE_GUID come from</span></div><div class="line" id="033446"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#033446" label="[03:34:46]">&lt;fossy_&gt;</a></span><span class="message">AHA!</span></div><div class="line" id="033520"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#033520" label="[03:35:20]">&lt;fossy_&gt;</a></span><span class="message">oh, this is very nice, got it to work :D</span></div><div class="line" id="033530"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#033530" label="[03:35:30]">&lt;fossy_&gt;</a></span><span class="message">(on one of my machines it wasn't working on)</span></div><div class="line" id="034324"><span class="nick" style="color:#389600"><a href="#034324" label="[03:43:24]">&lt;fossy_&gt;</a></span><span class="message">spec states that if you call get_variable with NULL data you must have 0 in data_size, which we were not doing</span></div><div class="line" id="052040"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#052040" label="[05:20:40]">&lt;fossy&gt;</a></span><span class="message">another thing getting a &quot;could not allocate memory for brk area&quot; when trying to use posix-runner.. any thoughts?</span></div><div class="line" id="060246"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#060246" label="[06:02:46]">&lt;fossy&gt;</a></span><span class="message"> <a rel="nofollow" href="https://0x0.st/X7bo.patch">https://0x0.st/X7bo.patch</a>  &lt;- relevant to earlier</span></div><div class="line" id="061925"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#061925" label="[06:19:25]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">i'm limited to 2Mbps uplink, without static-ip and without dns record (this requires expensive business contracting with the ISP which i cannot afford)</span></div><div class="line" id="061955"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#061955" label="[06:19:55]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">oriansj: seems a decent place if wherever your server is hosted got a little more bandwidth</span></div><div class="line" id="062102"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#062102" label="[06:21:02]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">i too notfied on tinycc mailing list, collecting regressions etc.; and some of this could easier be reproduced, tested and fixed if some prepared devdrop.iso was ready to boot</span></div><div class="line" id="062219"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#062219" label="[06:22:19]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">currently, the complete set of ~500ebuilds supported with tinycc including debugging symbols is 1.6GiB in size</span></div><div class="line" id="062340"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#062340" label="[06:23:40]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">i'll shrink that down to contain development utilities, and i have to rewrite the portage/ebuild scripting for re-integration into bootstrappable scripting</span></div><div class="line" id="072803"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#072803" label="[07:28:03]">&lt;fossy&gt;</a></span><span class="message">in other news, i am trying to decide a general rule of thumb for when to update toolchains in live-bootstrap....</span></div><div class="line" id="072941"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#072941" label="[07:29:41]">&lt;fossy&gt;</a></span><span class="message">considering following debian stable as a general baseline as a &quot;this is old but modern&quot;</span></div><div class="line" id="072958"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#072958" label="[07:29:58]">&lt;fossy&gt;</a></span><span class="message">but not &quot;too old that you'd need intermediate bootstrapping steps&quot;</span></div><div class="line" id="073121"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#073121" label="[07:31:21]">&lt;fossy&gt;</a></span><span class="message">because i want to avoid e.g. someone going to try to bootstrap debian from live-bootstrap as a base and running into reverse bootstrapping problems (trying to compile older GCC with new GCC can go poorly)</span></div><div class="line" id="074718"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#074718" label="[07:47:18]">&lt;jackdk&gt;</a></span><span class="message">Can you define some sort of &quot;layer&quot; or &quot;era&quot; abstraction, where you build up to a certain point before you hand off to the &quot;real&quot; distro to go the rest of the way?</span></div><div class="line" id="075414"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#075414" label="[07:54:14]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">some no-c++ system profile containing all development utilities, without any direct or transitive dependency towards, well, c++?</span></div><div class="line" id="075434"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#075434" label="[07:54:34]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">that includes almost all compilers/toolchains nowadays (because those are written in c++ themselves, llvm, gcc etc)</span></div><div class="line" id="080317"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#080317" label="[08:03:17]">&lt;fossy&gt;</a></span><span class="message">jackdk: that is.. a lot more difficult than i think one might consider</span></div><div class="line" id="080347"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#080347" label="[08:03:47]">&lt;fossy&gt;</a></span><span class="message">the hardest part of that is defining &quot;the rest of the way&quot;, how do you decide where to stop, and where to let the &quot;real&quot; distro continue from?</span></div><div class="line" id="080444"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#080444" label="[08:04:44]">&lt;fossy&gt;</a></span><span class="message">for me, out of the question is requiring distros to deal with anything historical, i.e. you need to go back in time for a rolling release distro, or use an old version of a versioned distro, in order to bootstrap from the output of live-bootstrap</span></div><div class="line" id="080517"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#080517" label="[08:05:17]">&lt;fossy&gt;</a></span><span class="message">to that end, aggi i have considerable issue with that, because that leaves us at ancient GCC, which is a non-starter</span></div><div class="line" id="080534"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#080534" label="[08:05:34]">&lt;fossy&gt;</a></span><span class="message">as you cannot realistically build any modern distros with that</span></div><div class="line" id="080726"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#080726" label="[08:07:26]">&lt;jackdk&gt;</a></span><span class="message">I'm sure it is =(. There's probably some uncontroversial part (up to at least a linux kernel and GCC 2.95 but probably 4 or even 10?) and then things get really fuzzy. If there's some definite bootstrapping path into the OS, then maybe you just need to be able to run that? For example nixos, which is what I currently know best, they've steadfastly stuck to nix-2.4 AIUI, so if you can build that, you can build a nixos and then lustrate into it</span></div><div class="line" id="080905"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#080905" label="[08:09:05]">&lt;fossy&gt;</a></span><span class="message">right. so GCC 10 is a bare minimum, as anything 13+ requires a minimum of 10 i think we found? or something like that, give or take a few versions</span></div><div class="line" id="081136"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#081136" label="[08:11:36]">&lt;fossy&gt;</a></span><span class="message">&quot;definite bootstrapping path into the OS, then maybe you just need to be able to run that?&quot; -- yeah, i'd tend to agree, i'm largely thinking about C/C++ toolchain, as pretty much every distro (exclude guix/nix(?)) do not have any kind of C bootstrap</span></div><div class="line" id="081227"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#081227" label="[08:12:27]">&lt;fossy&gt;</a></span><span class="message">maybe this is a much more difficult question to realise until people have attempted more distro bootstraps haha</span></div><div class="line" id="081434"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#081434" label="[08:14:34]">&lt;sam_&gt;</a></span><span class="message">15 needs 5.4 or so, 14 needs 4.8</span></div><div class="line" id="081442"><span class="nick" style="color:#80b1d3"><a href="#081442" label="[08:14:42]">&lt;sam_&gt;</a></span><span class="message">it's definitely not just a few versions</span></div><div class="line" id="081819"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#081819" label="[08:18:19]">&lt;jackdk&gt;</a></span><span class="message">nix will need C++. guix uses the nix daemon so probably will too. Debian will want to run `debootstrap` I suppose but if you're full-source bootstrapping `debootstrap` just to download binaries from the internet I notice that I am confused. (I suppose you can trust your gpg, at least)</span></div><div class="line" id="081940"><span class="nick" style="color:#6b8072"><a href="#081940" label="[08:19:40]">&lt;jackdk&gt;</a></span><span class="message">the equivalent in gentoo would be baking a stage1-equivalent tarball, I guess? Then you could unpack that into a chroot and build your gentoo?</span></div><div class="line" id="082131"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#082131" label="[08:21:31]">&lt;fossy&gt;</a></span><span class="message">sam_: hmmm, will need to check back on my notes/commit history, but there is some reason that in live-bootstrap we go 4.9-&gt;10-&gt;13 currently</span></div><div class="line" id="082214"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#082214" label="[08:22:14]">&lt;fossy&gt;</a></span><span class="message">ahhhh, okay, that was easy, because it's not 4.9, but 4.7 that we build currently</span></div><div class="line" id="082306"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#082306" label="[08:23:06]">&lt;fossy&gt;</a></span><span class="message">because that's the final C-only version, and then that can build up to GCC 10</span></div><div class="line" id="082336"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#082336" label="[08:23:36]">&lt;fossy&gt;</a></span><span class="message">sorry, had that around the wrong way</span></div><div class="line" id="082508"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#082508" label="[08:25:08]">&lt;fossy&gt;</a></span><span class="message">jackdk: yeah, we have had some progress on gentoo <a rel="nofollow" href="https://mid-kid.root.sx/git/mid-kid/bootstrap/src/branch/master/gentoo-2024/gentoo.txt">https://mid-kid.root.sx/git/mid-kid/bootstrap/src/branch/master/gentoo-2024/gentoo.txt</a> </span></div><div class="line" id="082519"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#082519" label="[08:25:19]">&lt;fossy&gt;</a></span><span class="message">i think there might have been a newer version of that somewhere</span></div><div class="line" id="082538"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#082538" label="[08:25:38]">&lt;fossy&gt;</a></span><span class="message">ah yep, Googulator's version: <a rel="nofollow" href="https://gist.github.com/Googulator/86af97ed078eb9e6c18c6eb49e46c96d">https://gist.github.com/Googulator/86af97ed078eb9e6c18c6eb49e46c96d</a> </span></div><div class="line" id="082558"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#082558" label="[08:25:58]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">fossy: it won't leave you at ancient GCC, if the dependency chain does not block you from compiling any recent toolchain, and tinycc can do just that.</span></div><div class="line" id="082630"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#082630" label="[08:26:30]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">so this would be another criteria, a complete system integration for a distribution driven by tinycc (or any other alternative complaint toolchain) would exist</span></div><div class="line" id="082646"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#082646" label="[08:26:46]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">*compliant</span></div><div class="line" id="082746"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#082746" label="[08:27:46]">&lt;fossy&gt;</a></span><span class="message">aggi: okay, i would like live-bootstrap to actually be usable though to bootstrap a modern distribution, and the reality is that everyone uses GCC or LLVM for their C compiler, and any modern GCC or LLVM needs C++</span></div><div class="line" id="082818"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#082818" label="[08:28:18]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">the integration path i would propose, for what jackd considers &quot;a certain point before you hand off to the real distro&quot;:</span></div><div class="line" id="082914"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#082914" label="[08:29:14]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">initial-seed/loaders -&gt; mesc -&gt; tcc-kernel/tcc-distro fully converged with live-bootstrap -&gt; any other toolchain/distro</span></div><div class="line" id="082950"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#082950" label="[08:29:50]">&lt;fossy&gt;</a></span><span class="message">in relation to this step, &quot;tcc-kernel/tcc-distro fully converged with live-bootstrap -&gt; any other toolchain/distro&quot;</span></div><div class="line" id="083009"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#083009" label="[08:30:09]">&lt;fossy&gt;</a></span><span class="message">any other toolchain/distro requires C++. tcc does not compile C++. how do you rectify that?</span></div><div class="line" id="083052"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#083052" label="[08:30:52]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">fossy: bootstrappable and too tcc already addressed the issue, because tcc can compile binutils and gcc-4.x, and from there you got various options to crossdev towards any architecture/toolchain desired</span></div><div class="line" id="083119"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#083119" label="[08:31:19]">&lt;fossy&gt;</a></span><span class="message">ohkay, this violates my requirement of live-bootstrap being directly able to build a modern distribution though</span></div><div class="line" id="083138"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#083138" label="[08:31:38]">&lt;fossy&gt;</a></span><span class="message">that's pretty much exactly what we already do in live-bootstrap</span></div><div class="line" id="083232"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#083232" label="[08:32:32]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">fossy: no, you haven't got a complete tcc distribution, which avoids gcc/binutils as mandatory dependency early in the dependency chain</span></div><div class="line" id="083258"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#083258" label="[08:32:58]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">and the question was, exactly when at which point system-integration was handed over to other distributions</span></div><div class="line" id="083413"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#083413" label="[08:34:13]">&lt;fossy&gt;</a></span><span class="message">fair. in this hypothetical situation, each distribution then needs to have a tinycc-&gt;modern toolchain bootstrap then, correct?</span></div><div class="line" id="083459"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#083459" label="[08:34:59]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">they always do, i would merely recommend a tinycc distribution existed for this, which was fully integrated with live-bootstrap</span></div><div class="line" id="083541"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#083541" label="[08:35:41]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">*i mean a complete tcc/linux distribution</span></div><div class="line" id="083637"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#083637" label="[08:36:37]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">live-bootstrap -&gt; i486-tcc-linux-musl.iso (without gcc) -&gt; any further system-integration</span></div><div class="line" id="083903"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#083903" label="[08:39:03]">&lt;fossy&gt;</a></span><span class="message">understood. i think what you are proposing is reasonable, but not what I want live-bootstrap to be. live-bootstrap's primary goal has always been a bootstrap of a &quot;modern toolchain&quot;, very roughly defined as capable of building LFS (or similar), and i would not consider tcc a &quot;modern toolchain&quot;</span></div><div class="line" id="083928"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#083928" label="[08:39:28]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">?</span></div><div class="line" id="084035"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#084035" label="[08:40:35]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">bootsrappable heavily relies upon tinycc, it could just simplify the whole transition to gcc/binutils/llvm if you got a bootable and complete development ISO with tinycc</span></div><div class="line" id="084112"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#084112" label="[08:41:12]">&lt;fossy&gt;</a></span><span class="message">i entirely agree - that's why i say i think your idea is reasonable - even that's a good idea - but i don't want live-bootstrap to stop there</span></div><div class="line" id="084120"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#084120" label="[08:41:20]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">with another benefit, gcc (and too binutils) were _optional_, not _mandatory_</span></div><div class="line" id="084201"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#084201" label="[08:42:01]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">i too agree, it must be possible to arrive at gcc and binutils, i would just prefer if that was optional for any distribution to decide</span></div><div class="line" id="084244"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#084244" label="[08:42:44]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">i too would prefer if Perl/autotools remained _optional_, but that's too much effort</span></div><div class="line" id="084305"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#084305" label="[08:43:05]">&lt;fossy&gt;</a></span><span class="message">sure. each distribution has to make a choice where they start their bootstrap</span></div><div class="line" id="084839"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#084839" label="[08:48:39]">&lt;fossy&gt;</a></span><span class="message">i have been thinking on and off that it might be nice to have different exit points from live-bootstrap, but that is not an easy thing to do properly</span></div><div class="line" id="085017"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#085017" label="[08:50:17]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">well, i think it is feasible to emit a complete i486-tcc-linux-musl.iso fully converged and re-integrated with live-bootstrap</span></div><div class="line" id="085051"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#085051" label="[08:50:51]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">although this required a &quot;little&quot; fork, and i am overwhelmed already with forking against linux-2.4, and forking against gentoo already</span></div><div class="line" id="085142"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#085142" label="[08:51:42]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">keeping tinycc synced to latest HEAD, was, well another little headache with regressions introduced</span></div><div class="line" id="085321"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#085321" label="[08:53:21]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">and, worst of it, i had proposed this project work to various employers in germany and a faculty, which was ignored</span></div><div class="line" id="085351"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#085351" label="[08:53:51]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">besides i got banned from #gentoo-toolchain and #gentoo-releng</span></div><div class="line" id="085515"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#085515" label="[08:55:15]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">last wednesday i asked at financial department if some ISP business contracting could be supported, so i could host my stuff, ... denied</span></div><div class="line" id="095258"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#095258" label="[09:52:58]">&lt;fossy&gt;</a></span><span class="message">can you elaborate on what &quot;fully converged and re-integrated&quot; means?</span></div><div class="line" id="095408"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#095408" label="[09:54:08]">&lt;fossy&gt;</a></span><span class="message">i think this would be a very difficult project to get funded, lol</span></div><div class="line" id="095444"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#095444" label="[09:54:44]">&lt;fossy&gt;</a></span><span class="message">in the long term, i would personally prefer tinycc to be removed from the bootstrap chain, it is one of the greatest pain points currently</span></div><div class="line" id="095507"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#095507" label="[09:55:07]">&lt;fossy&gt;</a></span><span class="message">... how did you manage to get banned??</span></div><div class="line" id="100143"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#100143" label="[10:01:43]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">i got banned when discussing tinycc-toolchain integration into gentoo</span></div><div class="line" id="100301"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#100301" label="[10:03:01]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">fossy: this means some packaging/scripting is necessary, and live-bootstrap style seems a decent approach to replace portage/ebuilds</span></div><div class="line" id="100344"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#100344" label="[10:03:44]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">hence, i can follow the live-guistrap path up until some tinycc and maybe as86 assembler exist, then emit tccboot loader to compiler a linux-2.x kernel, and a sufficiently complete devutils-root.squashfs loaded</span></div><div class="line" id="100356"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#100356" label="[10:03:56]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">*live-bootstrap</span></div><div class="line" id="100535"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#100535" label="[10:05:35]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">and i consider tinycc support some of the biggest benefits, since the bigger &quot;pain points&quot; are the mandatory gcc/binutils dependency far too early during bootstrapping</span></div><div class="line" id="100717"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#100717" label="[10:07:17]">&lt;fossy&gt;</a></span><span class="message">ok final question for you for now, what's the concern with gcc/binutils?</span></div><div class="line" id="100939"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#100939" label="[10:09:39]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">fossy: first and foremost, it isn't their GPL licensing, but technical issues, which is why i consider those developement tools inappropriate when those become a _mandatory_ dependency far too early during bootstrapping</span></div><div class="line" id="101021"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#101021" label="[10:10:21]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">both gcc and binutils are gigantic in size, lines of code, it's too much depending on circuumstances</span></div><div class="line" id="101049"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#101049" label="[10:10:49]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">and in practice, tinycc is much faster compile-time, which greatly improved my workflow ever since i got full support with my system profile for it</span></div><div class="line" id="101332"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#101332" label="[10:13:32]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">and i've seen gcc hitting oom with 4GiB RAM available far too often</span></div><div class="line" id="101510"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#101510" label="[10:15:10]">&lt;fossy&gt;</a></span><span class="message">&quot;circumstances&quot; must be pretty minimal requirements... no chance you can use most useful modern software with only tcc</span></div><div class="line" id="101601"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#101601" label="[10:16:01]">&lt;fossy&gt;</a></span><span class="message">faster compile-time i find to be a largely non-issue, especially as it generates slower (less optimized) code than gcc/llvm, but i see the benefit for development in some situations</span></div><div class="line" id="101609"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#101609" label="[10:16:09]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">that's the trick, i don't need to; and it is possible to optionally spawn any recent toolchain with tinycc</span></div><div class="line" id="101628"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#101628" label="[10:16:28]">&lt;fossy&gt;</a></span><span class="message">what are you compiling with tcc that gcc uses 4GB of RAM for, lol</span></div><div class="line" id="101656"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#101656" label="[10:16:56]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">fossy: for example i bootstrapped a gcc-6.x with gcc-4.x, &quot;lol&quot;</span></div><div class="line" id="101735"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#101735" label="[10:17:35]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">because i had covered aarch64 with my crossdev stuff, that needs a latest toolchain</span></div><div class="line" id="101802"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#101802" label="[10:18:02]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">which, admittedly, is a disadvantage with tcc, it cannot and maybe should not cover all ISAs</span></div><div class="line" id="101835"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#101835" label="[10:18:35]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">with compile-time/run-time performance tradeoff tinycc is far closer to the sweet spot than gcc or llvm could ever be</span></div><div class="line" id="101931"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#101931" label="[10:19:31]">&lt;fossy&gt;</a></span><span class="message">maybe a good performance tradeoff for the &lt;1% of users who compile their own software on low-mid spec machines :)</span></div><div class="line" id="101958"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#101958" label="[10:19:58]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">tinycc compile-time is much(!) faster an all machines</span></div><div class="line" id="102206"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#102206" label="[10:22:06]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">and important, i would be far less excited about tinycc, if it wasn't possible to spawn a gcc/binutils with it, but thankfully it is</span></div><div class="line" id="102218"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#102218" label="[10:22:18]">&lt;fossy&gt;</a></span><span class="message">yes, but for anyone who's not building for just themselves, and not using incremental compilation, i.e. distributions/businesses, they care a lot more about runtime speed, ISA support, and quantity of software buildable when they choose a compiler, than runtime speed</span></div><div class="line" id="102224"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#102224" label="[10:22:24]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">tinycc does NOT lock you out from transitioning towards a full GNU</span></div><div class="line" id="102304"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#102304" label="[10:23:04]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">with regards to runtime performance, another hint, i think a linux-2.x is more efficient, if it had full support for NPTL and O1 scheduler</span></div><div class="line" id="102323"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#102323" label="[10:23:23]">&lt;fossy&gt;</a></span><span class="message">linux-2.x is more efficient than modern kernels??</span></div><div class="line" id="102330"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#102330" label="[10:23:30]">&lt;fossy&gt;</a></span><span class="message">modern Linux kernels*</span></div><div class="line" id="102407"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#102407" label="[10:24:07]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">yes, i think so, regardless of BKL, i think it is missing O1 scheduler and NPTL which weren't rebased onto linux-2.4.37.11 which severly limited scalability of it</span></div><div class="line" id="102442"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#102442" label="[10:24:42]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">in parallel linux rushed towards their 2.6 series kernel, which quadrupled lines of code.. something i dislike in principle</span></div><div class="line" id="102513"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#102513" label="[10:25:13]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">otherwise i would invest more time and effort into supporting a later kernel version, but i think it is not worth it due to this</span></div><div class="line" id="102621"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#102621" label="[10:26:21]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">a problem major distros have, they cannot roll-back kernel or toolchain, optionally at least, they can't</span></div><div class="line" id="102622"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#102622" label="[10:26:22]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">i can</span></div><div class="line" id="102630"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#102630" label="[10:26:30]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">and i will</span></div><div class="line" id="102735"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#102735" label="[10:27:35]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">bootstrappable.org chose a 4.x kernel version, which is missing time64 i think, hence i see no relevant disadvantage to roll-back further</span></div><div class="line" id="102752"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#102752" label="[10:27:52]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">*for POSIX 32bit, which is the basis of bootstrapping, indefinitely</span></div><div class="line" id="102753"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#102753" label="[10:27:53]">&lt;fossy&gt;</a></span><span class="message">answer to that is not difficult: hardware support.</span></div><div class="line" id="102825"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#102825" label="[10:28:25]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">see, linux-2.4 seems to hit another sweet spot, because usb-2.x, SMP, PAE are feasible</span></div><div class="line" id="102911"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#102911" label="[10:29:11]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">usb-2.x and PAE already booted, SMP is a little flaky when compiling with tinycc still</span></div><div class="line" id="102929"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#102929" label="[10:29:29]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">i already could boot a kernel patched for o1/ntpl too, that's flaky too</span></div><div class="line" id="103005"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#103005" label="[10:30:05]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">anyway, linux-2.x was the better kernel imo</span></div><div class="line" id="103014"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#103014" label="[10:30:14]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">and tinycc seems the better toolchain</span></div><div class="line" id="103019"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#103019" label="[10:30:19]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">by design</span></div><div class="line" id="103040"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#103040" label="[10:30:40]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">i've not checked GNU hurd yet though, but i suspect it may not have USB support anyway</span></div><div class="line" id="103101"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#103101" label="[10:31:01]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">as far as &quot;kernel bootstrapping&quot; was concerned, and which options exist</span></div><div class="line" id="103135"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#103135" label="[10:31:35]">&lt;fossy&gt;</a></span><span class="message">NVME, AHCI, EFI, any still usable non-i[3456]86 ISA, USB-C, are all missing from 2.4 at least</span></div><div class="line" id="103154"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#103154" label="[10:31:54]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">ahci was unstable, yes, but it already got introduced</span></div><div class="line" id="103224"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#103224" label="[10:32:24]">&lt;fossy&gt;</a></span><span class="message">anyways, i need to go do other things, so i will leave our discussion here, but overall i don't think your philosophy, even if correct from a technical basis (which i find dubious), is really usable by most people for any real work</span></div><div class="line" id="103235"><span class="nick" style="color:#8dd3c7"><a href="#103235" label="[10:32:35]">&lt;fossy&gt;</a></span><span class="message">in 2024</span></div><div class="line" id="103245"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#103245" label="[10:32:45]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">sata support is present, but not for all controllers, and i see no *practical* benefit, an NVME can be exposed as IDE device too</span></div><div class="line" id="103320"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#103320" label="[10:33:20]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">yes, linux-2.4 maintenance would need siginifcant efforts for backporting some stuff</span></div><div class="line" id="103346"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#103346" label="[10:33:46]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">for example, it hasn't got a 32bit POSIX filesystem with 64bit timestamps, and nilfs2 was introduced with linux-2.5/6 series</span></div><div class="line" id="103407"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#103407" label="[10:34:07]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">i _must_ backport nilfs2, and a few other things (usb-audio, usb-ethernet)</span></div><div class="line" id="103436"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#103436" label="[10:34:36]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">for _long term_ support</span></div><div class="line" id="103441"><span class="nick" style="color:#6d2462"><a href="#103441" label="[10:34:41]">&lt;matrix_bridge&gt;</a></span><span class="message">&lt;cosinusoidally&gt; fossy: I found that live-bootstrap under a 2.6 kernel was actually faster than under a 5.15 kernel, so newer is not always faster <a rel="nofollow" href="https://logs.guix.gnu.org/bootstrappable/2024-11-16.log#153309">https://logs.guix.gnu.org/bootstrappable/2024-11-16.log#153309</a>  but it probably depend on workload.</span></div><div class="line" id="103445"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#103445" label="[10:34:45]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">beyond year2038</span></div><div class="line" id="105654"><span class="nick" style="color:#234e69"><a href="#105654" label="[10:56:54]">&lt;Googulator&gt;</a></span><span class="message">I've not heard of any NVMe that can emulate IDE - but even if it can be done, IDE emulation on modern CPUs is problematic</span></div><div class="line" id="105721"><span class="nick" style="color:#234e69"><a href="#105721" label="[10:57:21]">&lt;Googulator&gt;</a></span><span class="message">IDE uses legacy x86 port-mapped IO</span></div><div class="line" id="105741"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#105741" label="[10:57:41]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">*sata</span></div><div class="line" id="105803"><span class="nick" style="color:#234e69"><a href="#105803" label="[10:58:03]">&lt;Googulator&gt;</a></span><span class="message">That's AHCI</span></div><div class="line" id="105822"><span class="nick" style="color:#234e69"><a href="#105822" label="[10:58:22]">&lt;Googulator&gt;</a></span><span class="message">Non-AHCI SATA is just IDE emulation, and it has the same PMIO issue</span></div><div class="line" id="105834"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#105834" label="[10:58:34]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">yes, but the choice to begin with, is USB2.x or none thereof at all with any kernel-version chose</span></div><div class="line" id="105845"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#105845" label="[10:58:45]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">fiwix, gnu hurd, linux-2.x being candidates</span></div><div class="line" id="105855"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#105855" label="[10:58:55]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">n</span></div><div class="line" id="105936"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#105936" label="[10:59:36]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">minix</span></div><div class="line" id="110006"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#110006" label="[11:00:06]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">some minix guy had joined discussion on tinycc mailing list</span></div><div class="line" id="110012"><span class="nick" style="color:#234e69"><a href="#110012" label="[11:00:12]">&lt;Googulator&gt;</a></span><span class="message">Also, being tcc-compilable is not enough for kernel bootstrap</span></div><div class="line" id="110031"><span class="nick" style="color:#234e69"><a href="#110031" label="[11:00:31]">&lt;Googulator&gt;</a></span><span class="message">Any kernel used needs to be tcc-compilable *on builder-hex0*</span></div><div class="line" id="110142"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#110142" label="[11:01:42]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">you need to distinguish compatibility for the use-case, that was either merely bootstrapping, or to emite a _complete_ distribution</span></div><div class="line" id="110219"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#110219" label="[11:02:19]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">i think it is possible to keep a clean dependency chain from build-hex0 towards tccboot/kernel and some devutil.squashfs loaded with it then</span></div><div class="line" id="110319"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#110319" label="[11:03:19]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">at the stage of hex0 and later mesc/M2-planet, there isn't a bare-metal bootable distro yet, but it contains the needed utilities to emit some</span></div><div class="line" id="110402"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#110402" label="[11:04:02]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">a major caveat seems to be 16bit real-mode asm processing, as86 and legacy linux-2.2 bootcode.S stuff might help</span></div><div class="line" id="110417"><span class="nick" style="color:#2e2a4a"><a href="#110417" label="[11:04:17]">&lt;aggi&gt;</a></span><span class="message">so all this doesn't need to be re-written from scratch</span></div><div class="line" id="111354"><span class="nick" style="color:#6d2462"><a href="#111354" label="[11:13:54]">&lt;matrix_bridge&gt;</a></span><span class="message">&lt;cosinusoidally&gt; builder-hex0 -&gt;  tcc -&gt; tcc-linux might be possible (similar to how live-bootstrap currently does builder-hex0 -&gt; tcc -&gt; fiwix). It may be easier to keep the fiwix step though, just for ease of use/easier debugging/testing.</span></div><div class="line" id="111429"><span class="nick" style="color:#6d2462"><a href="#111429" label="[11:14:29]">&lt;matrix_bridge&gt;</a></span><span class="message">&lt;cosinusoidally&gt; fiwix could even be used to set up the hard drive before building/installing tcc-linux.</span></div><div class="line" id="111633"><span class="nick" style="color:#6d2462"><a href="#111633" label="[11:16:33]">&lt;matrix_bridge&gt;</a></span><span class="message">&lt;cosinusoidally&gt; or is there some flakeyness around hard drive access from fiwix in the live-bootstrap version? I know you do the fiwix -&gt; linux step in ram via kexec.</span></div><div class="line" id="111720"><span class="nick" style="color:#6d2462"><a href="#111720" label="[11:17:20]">&lt;matrix_bridge&gt;</a></span><span class="message">&lt;cosinusoidally&gt; linux-tcc I mean</span></div><div class="line" id="111852"><span class="nick" style="color:#234e69"><a href="#111852" label="[11:18:52]">&lt;Googulator&gt;</a></span><span class="message">Fiwix only supports legacy IDE-style hard drive access AFAIK</span></div><div class="line" id="115035"><span class="nick" style="color:#6c3d55"><a href="#115035" label="[11:50:35]">&lt;Mikaku&gt;</a></span><span class="message">Fiwix currently supports PIO and MWDMA (only QEMU Intel PIIX3 PCI ISA IDE controller) ATA modes for hard disk access</span></div><div class="line" id="120845"><span class="nick" style="color:#234e69"><a href="#120845" label="[12:08:45]">&lt;Googulator&gt;</a></span><span class="message">I mean legacy IDE as in non AHCI or anything that's exclusively MMIO</span></div><div class="line" id="120905"><span class="nick" style="color:#234e69"><a href="#120905" label="[12:09:05]">&lt;Googulator&gt;</a></span><span class="message">the use of PMIO is what makes it legacy</span></div><div class="line" id="120947"><span class="nick" style="color:#234e69"><a href="#120947" label="[12:09:47]">&lt;Googulator&gt;</a></span><span class="message">because the concept of &quot;IO ports&quot; never existed outside of x86, and is problematic even on modern x86</span></div><div class="line" id="121058"><span class="nick" style="color:#234e69"><a href="#121058" label="[12:10:58]">&lt;Googulator&gt;</a></span><span class="message">Intel VT, AMD-V and ACPI all have hacks to deal with PMIO that unfortunately require OS support</span></div><div class="line" id="121334"><span class="nick" style="color:#6c3d55"><a href="#121334" label="[12:13:34]">&lt;Mikaku&gt;</a></span><span class="message">AHCI is indeed not supported yet</span></div><br /></div></body></html>